# ============================================================================
# GramFinance environment template
# ============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env.local` is git-ignored; never commit a
# filled-in copy of this file.
#
# Everything the application actually reads is listed below, and nothing else is
# listed as if it were required. The read site is noted for each variable so you
# can confirm what a value does.
# ============================================================================


# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Supabase project URL. Read by `lib/supabase/env.ts`.
# The app renders without it (every feature degrades gracefully), but auth,
# schemes, fraud and feedback all stop working.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# Supabase ANON key -- the public, RLS-respecting key. Read by
# `lib/supabase/env.ts`. This is safe to expose to the browser; that is what RLS
# is for.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key-here

# Google Gemini API key. Read by `lib/ai/gemini.ts`, server-side only.
# Without it `POST /api/assistant` answers 503 rather than 500. The key is never
# logged and never leaves the server.
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key-here


# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which Gemini model the assistant uses. Read by `lib/ai/gemini.ts`.
# Defaults to `gemini-3.5-flash` when unset, so you only need this to pin or
# change the model.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash


# ── Declared but NOT read by the application ────────────────────────────────
# Listed so nobody goes looking for them, and so nobody adds a "fix" that wires
# them up. Neither is referenced anywhere in `app/`, `lib/` or `features/`.

# Absolute origin of the deployment. Unused today: nothing reads it, and no
# feature needs a canonical absolute URL. Set it only when a feature actually
# requires one (for example email or OAuth callbacks).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase SERVICE ROLE key. Deliberately unused, and there is no service-role
# client anywhere in this project.
#
# This key bypasses RLS entirely: anything built with it can read and write every
# row in the database regardless of policy. `lib/supabase/env.ts` excludes it on
# purpose and a guard test enforces that it stays excluded. Every current data
# path -- seed data, admin tooling -- runs through a privileged session outside
# the application, so there is nothing here for it to do.
#
# Setting it here would not be exploited, but it would put a database-wide
# bypass credential into every developer's local environment and every deploy
# target's config, and it would sit one careless `import` away from the browser
# bundle. Leave it unset. If a future feature seems to need it, that is a signal
# to fix the RLS policy or the feature design, not to add the key.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ── Supabase project configuration (Dashboard, not this file) ──────────────
# Two project-level switches live in the Supabase Dashboard, not in the
# environment, and the app silently fails without them:
#
#   Authentication -> Providers -> Anonymous Sign-Ins  must be ENABLED.
#     Without it `POST /api/auth/sign-in` answers 503 AUTH_UNAVAILABLE and the
#     live auth tests skip themselves with that reason.
#
#   site_url under Authentication -> URL Configuration must match the real
#     deployment origin. `supabase/config.toml` still carries a TODO for this
#     because no deployment target has been chosen yet.
#
# For local development with the Supabase CLI, `supabase start` reads
# `supabase/config.toml` instead, which already has anonymous sign-ins enabled.